<% title "Payout account — #{@event.name}" %>

<%= render "events/nav", selected: :payout_method %>

<h1 class="heading">Payout account</h1>

<%# Fuime: where this venture's money gets sent.

    Under merchant-of-record there is no merchant account for an operator to
    open — the money is Fuime's own revenue from Fuime's own sale, and what this
    operator holds is a payable. So the only thing left to ask for is somewhere
    to send their share, and it is asked when there is money to send rather than
    before they have made a sale (Fuime::PayoutMethod, MOR_MIGRATION_PLAN §8.3b).

    TWO AUDIENCES on one page, the same split as the Payments screen it replaces:
    the whole team reads it, because a teen needs to know whether their venture
    can be paid; only the responsible adult acts, because the bank account is
    theirs and because a teen who could repoint the destination could route their
    own earnings past the approval that makes the ownership structure real (L2).

    Above everything: a venture that cannot sell has a more urgent problem than
    how it gets paid. Renders nothing when clear. %>
<%= render "fuime/selling_blockers", event: @event %>

<%# ── What is at stake, in dollars ─────────────────────────────────────────────

    The honest argument for doing this, and the reason the page leads with it.
    Under MoR, Fuime::PayableAssessment SKIPS a venture with no verified
    destination from every payout run — so an operator with money owed and no
    bank connected is not waiting, they are stuck, and nothing else on this page
    would tell them that.

    Read through Fuime::PayablesLedger like every other operator-facing figure;
    spec/views/fuime/payables_copy_spec.rb enforces that this is the only thing a
    view may ask, and that this is never called a balance. %>
<% if @payables.amount_owed_cents.positive? %>
  <p class="h2 mt2 mb0"><%= render_money(@payables.amount_owed_cents) %></p>
  <p class="secondary mt0"><%= @payables.owed_sentence %></p>
<% end %>

<% if !@configured %>
  <%# Fuime has no Plaid keys. An operator problem and not the family's, so it
      says so plainly rather than blaming the bank — telling a teenager their
      bank refused them when Fuime never had an API key is a lie with a support
      ticket attached. %>
  <%= render "application/callout", type: "warning", title: "Connecting a bank isn't available yet" do %>
    <p class="mb-0">
      We're still setting this up. Nothing is wrong with your account — check
      back shortly, and <%= help_message %>
    </p>
  <% end %>
<% end %>

<% if @payout_method.present? %>
  <h2 class="mt2">Your payout account</h2>

  <table class="table--autosize">
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>Account</td>
        <td><strong><%= @payout_method.display_name %></strong></td>
      </tr>
      <% if @payout_method.account_holder_name.present? %>
        <tr>
          <%# The name the BANK reports, not one anybody typed. Shown because a
              destination in a third party's name is the thing a payout review
              exists to catch, and the person best placed to notice it is the
              adult reading this page. %>
          <td>In the name of</td>
          <td><%= @payout_method.account_holder_name %></td>
        </tr>
      <% end %>
      <tr>
        <td>Status</td>
        <td>
          <% if @payout_method.verified? %>
            <span class="badge bg-success">Verified</span>
            <% if @payout_method.verified_at.present? %>
              <span class="secondary">— connected <%= @payout_method.verified_at.strftime("%B %-d, %Y") %></span>
            <% end %>
          <% elsif @payout_method.failed? %>
            <span class="badge bg-error">Couldn't be verified</span>
            <% if @payout_method.failure_reason.present? %>
              <span class="secondary">— <%= @payout_method.failure_reason %></span>
            <% end %>
          <% else %>
            <span class="badge bg-info">Not verified yet</span>
          <% end %>
        </td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <%# The two claims this page could make falsely, both guarded.

      A row that is not `verified` is not a payout destination — PayableAssessment
      reads `usable`, which is verified only — so saying "you're all set" on a
      pending row would tell somebody they were connected while every run silently
      skipped them. And under Connect the destination is not read at all, because
      the money is already in the family's own Stripe account. %>
  <% if !@payout_method.usable? %>
    <p class="secondary">
      This account isn't verified, so payouts can't be sent to it yet. Connecting
      it again is the fastest way to fix that.
    </p>
  <% elsif !@in_use %>
    <p class="secondary">
      This venture is still paid through its own Stripe account, so nothing will
      be sent here yet. We've saved it for when that changes.
    </p>
  <% else %>
    <p class="secondary">Payouts are sent here.</p>
  <% end %>

<% elsif @configured %>
  <h2 class="mt2">Connect where you'd like to be paid</h2>

  <% if @in_use %>
    <p>
      Fuime pays what you're owed to a bank account you choose. Until one is
      connected, your earnings stay recorded here and aren't included in a payout
      run.
    </p>
  <% else %>
    <p>
      Connect a bank account now and it'll be ready when Fuime starts paying
      earnings out directly. For the moment this venture is still paid through
      its own Stripe account.
    </p>
  <% end %>
<% end %>

<%# ── The action ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

    Adult only. A teen sees everything above and is told who to ask, rather than
    being shown a button that would refuse them — a disabled control with no
    explanation reads as a broken page to the person it is aimed at. %>
<% if @can_connect && @configured %>
  <div data-controller="plaid-link"
       data-plaid-link-token-url-value="<%= fuime_payout_method_link_token_path(event_slug: @event.slug) %>">
    <button class="btn bg-accent"
            data-action="plaid-link#open"
            data-plaid-link-target="button"
            data-label="<%= @payout_method.present? ? "Connect a different account" : "Connect a bank account" %>">
      <%= @payout_method.present? ? "Connect a different account" : "Connect a bank account" %>
    </button>

    <%# What Plaid Link hands back, exchanged server-side. Never an account
        number: these two values are a one-time token and an opaque account id,
        and Fuime::PlaidLinkService is written so the digits cannot be read even
        deliberately. %>
    <%= form_with url: fuime_payout_method_create_path(event_slug: @event.slug),
                  method: :post, data: { plaid_link_target: "form", turbo: false } do |f| %>
      <%= f.hidden_field :public_token, data: { plaid_link_target: "publicToken" } %>
      <%= f.hidden_field :account_id, data: { plaid_link_target: "accountId" } %>
    <% end %>

    <p class="error mt1" data-plaid-link-target="error" hidden></p>
  </div>

  <p class="secondary mt1">
    You'll sign in to your bank through Plaid. Fuime never sees your username,
    your password, or your account number — we're given a token that says the
    account is yours, and that's all we store.
  </p>

  <% if @payout_method.present? %>
    <%# Removal, kept well away from the connect button. Retiring the only
        destination stops payouts, so it asks first and says what stops. %>
    <p class="mt2">
      <%= button_to "Remove this account",
                    fuime_payout_method_remove_path(event_slug: @event.slug, id: @payout_method.id),
                    method: :delete,
                    class: "btn bg-error",
                    data: { turbo_confirm: "Remove #{@payout_method.display_name}? " \
                                           "Payouts can't be sent until another account is connected."
} %>
    </p>
  <% end %>
<% elsif !@can_connect %>
  <p class="secondary mt2">
    <% if @event.institutionally_sponsored? %>
      Your school sets up where this venture's money is sent.
    <% elsif @responsible_adults.any? %>
      <%= @responsible_adults.map { |g| g.name.presence || g.email }.to_sentence %>
      can connect the bank account this gets paid into. It has to be an adult's
      account, so it's theirs to set up.
    <% else %>
      A parent or guardian connects the bank account this gets paid into. There
      isn't an active guardian on this venture yet.
      <%= link_to "Invite your guardian", new_guardianship_path, class: "underline" %>.
    <% end %>
  </p>
<% end %>

<%# ── Sandbox disclosure ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────

    Same posture as the Stripe test-mode banners shipped in Phase 1: the app runs
    against a test environment and nothing said so, on a page asking a stranger
    for a real detail. A Plaid sandbox connection accepts fake bank logins and
    connects nothing real, and a family must not be able to believe otherwise.
    Reads the environment, so pointing at production removes it. %>
<% if @sandbox %>
  <%= render "application/callout", type: "info", title: "This is a test connection" do %>
    <p class="mb-0">
      Fuime is running against Plaid's test environment, so this connects a
      practice bank rather than a real one. Nothing you connect here can send or
      receive real money.
    </p>
  <% end %>
<% end %>

<%# Standing disclosure. Fuime is not a bank and does not hold this money — it is
    sent to an account the family owns (L5). %>
<p class="secondary mt2">
  Fuime is a financial technology company, not a bank. Money is sent to the bank
  account connected here, which is owned by the parent, guardian or school — not
  by Fuime.
</p>
